<td>0.27</td><td>27.38</td><td>0.52</td><td>21.10</td><td>2.45</td><td>24.79</td><td>1.31</td><td>38.39</td><td>2.49</td><td>32.56</td><td>0.12</td><td>31.53</td><td>0.12</td><td>21.29</td><td>1.68</td><td>33.56+2.37</td><td>0.87+0.01</td><td>20.44</td><td>2.29</td><td>36.51</td><td>1.23</td><td>41.07</td><td>2.41</td><td>31.81</td><td>0.14</td><td>33.09</td><td>0.12</td><td>20.71</td><td>1.85</td><td>34.91+3.72</td><td>0.85-0.01</td></tr><tr><td>AQD (AFC)</td><td>57.34</td><td>0.14</td><td>43.05</td><td>0.14</td><td>35.80</td><td>0.25</td><td>31.27</td><td>0.26</td><td>29.03</td><td>0.50</td><td>20.44</td><td>2.29</td><td>37.91</td><td>1.23</td><td>49.67</td><td>2.41</td><td>33.19</td><td>0.14</td><td>31.77</td><td>0.12</td><td>24.86</td><td>1.85</td><td>37.48+6.29</td><td>0.85-0.01</td><td>20.44</td><td>2.29</td><td>37.91</td><td>1.23</td><td>49.67</td><td>2.41</td><td>33.19</td><td>0.14</td><td>33.09</td><td>0.12</td><td>24.86</td><td>1.85</td><td>37.48+6.29</td><td>0.85-0.01</td></tr></table>", "block_bbox": [111, 610, 1104, 715], "block_id": 8, "block_order": null, "group_id": 8, "block_polygon_points": [[111.0, 610.0], [1104.0, 610.0], [1104.0, 715.0], [111.0, 715.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "7.3.1 Performance in Effectiveness. We first use QD-GNN to replace the GNN model in ICS-GNN framework for non-attributed community search. As shown in Table 3, QD-GNN outperforms the original ICS-GNN in all data sets with 2.37% improvement in F1-score. We also use AQD-GNN to replace the GNN model in ICS-GNN so it supports interactive attributed community search. As shown in Table 3, AQD-GNN further improves the F1-score of the original ICS-GNN for all data sets by 3.72% (AFN) and 6.29% (AFC) on average. This experiment proves that our QD-GNN and AQD-GNN models are more effective than Vanilla GCN in the ICS-GNN framework.", "block_bbox": [100, 1039, 594, 1281], "block_id": 9, "block_order": 1, "group_id": 9, "block_polygon_points": [[100.0, 1039.0], [594.0, 1039.0], [594.0, 1281.0], [100.0, 1281.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "7.3.2 Performance in Efficiency. We report the average time of community search per interaction by ICS-GNN, QD-GNN and AQD-GNN in Table 3. The running time of the three models are very close. Without increasing the time cost, we improve the performance of ICS-GNN and extend it to support attributed interactive community search problem.", "block_bbox": [100, 1289, 595, 1422], "block_id": 10, "block_order": 2, "group_id": 10, "block_polygon_points": [[100.0, 1289.0], [595.0, 1289.0], [595.0, 1422.0], [100.0, 1422.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "the user is satisfied. In each interaction, ICS-GNN first finds a candidate subgraph, learns the vertex embedding through a Vanilla GCN model [27] and finally employs a BFS based algorithm to select k-sized community with the maximum GNN scores. Note that ICS-GNN does not use any training queries with ground-truth communities to train the model; for each user query, it re-trains the GNN model to obtain the vertices' embeddings only from the knowledge of the given query. ICS-GNN only supports non-attributed community search.", "block_bbox": [101, 746, 594, 941], "block_id": 11, "block_order": 3, "group_id": 11, "block_polygon_points": [[101.0, 746.0], [594.0, 746.0], [594.0, 941.0], [101.0, 941.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "In this experiment, we replace the Vanilla GCN model [27] in the ICS-GNN [14] with our community search models QD-GNN and AQD-GNN to compare the performance of interactive community search problem.", "block_bbox": [101, 943, 592, 1030], "block_id": 12, "block_order": 4, "group_id": 12, "block_polygon_points": [[101.0, 943.0], [592.0, 943.0], [592.0, 1030.0], [101.0, 1030.0]]}, {"block_label": "figure_title", "block_content": "Table 4: The performances of ACS methods on large data sets.", "block_bbox": [630, 741, 1118, 761], "block_id": 13, "block_order": null, "group_id": 13, "block_polygon_points": [[630.0, 741.0], [1117.0, 741.0], [1117.0, 760.0], [630.0, 760.0]]}, {"block_label": "table", "block_content": "<table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Methods</td><td colspan=\"3\">Reddit</td><td colspan=\"3\">Enlarged_Reddit</td></tr><tr><td>Index/Train Time</td><td>Query Time</td><td>F1-score</td><td>Index/Train Time</td><td>Query Time</td><td>F1-score</td></tr><tr><td>ACQ</td><td>42.4 s</td><td>32.2 ms</td><td>0.53</td><td>852.7 s</td><td>5726.6 ms $ ^{*} $</td><td>0.38</td></tr><tr><td>ATC $ ^{\\#} $</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>AQD-GNN</td><td>4993.6 s</td><td>6.7 ms</td><td>0.91</td><td>3898.5 s</td><td>5.3 ms</td><td>0.91</td></tr></table>", "block_bbox": [648, 773, 1099, 873], "block_id": 14, "block_order": null, "group_id": 14, "block_polygon_points": [[648.0, 773.0], [1099.0, 773.0], [1099.0, 873.0], [648.0, 873.0]]}, {"block_label": "vision_footnote", "block_content": "25 out of 100 queries are out of memory when processing. The query time are the average of the rest 75 queries.", "block_bbox": [661, 876, 1100, 902], "block_id": 15, "block_order": null, "group_id": 15, "block_polygon_points": [[661.0, 876.0], [1100.0, 876.0], [1100.0, 902.0], [661.0, 902.0]]}, {"block_label": "vision_footnote", "block_content": " $ ^{*} $ ATC did not finish building its index in 7 days on both two data sets.", "block_bbox": [654, 905, 1063, 921], "block_id": 16, "block_order": null, "group_id": 16, "block_polygon_points": [[654.0, 905.0], [1062.0, 905.0], [1062.0, 920.0], [654.0, 920.0]]}, {"block_label": "paragraph_title", "block_content": "7.4 ACS on Large Graphs", "block_bbox": [631, 957, 899, 979], "block_id": 17, "block_order": 5, "group_id": 17, "block_polygon_points": [[631.0, 957.0], [899.0, 957.0], [899.0, 979.0], [631.0, 979.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "In this experiment, we evaluate the performance of our model for ACS on large graphs. We design a subgraph training mechanism to train our models on large graphs. We first select neighbors of query vertices as the candidate subgraph for each query. According to the number of neighbors, we select 1 or 2-hop neighbors in the fusion graph described in Section 6.6. Then we train our model on these small subgraphs and predict communities.", "block_bbox": [629, 983, 1121, 1136], "block_id": 18, "block_order": 6, "group_id": 18, "block_polygon_points": [[629.0, 983.0], [1121.0, 983.0], [1121.0, 1136.0], [629.0, 1136.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "We compare AQD-GNN with ACQ and ATC for attributed community search on Reddit and an enlarged version of Reddit, denoted as Enlarged_Reddit. To enlarge Reddit and preserve the ground-truth communities at the same time, we add some new vertices for edges within a community. A new vertex is linked to the two ends of an edge, and the attributes of the new vertex are the average attribute values of the two ends. The Enlarged_Reddit has 3.12M vertices and 126M edges.", "block_bbox": [629, 1137, 1122, 1310], "block_id": 19, "block_order": 7, "group_id": 19, "block_polygon_points": [[629.0, 1137.0], [1122.0, 1137.0], [1122.0, 1310.0], [629.0, 1310.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "Table 4 reports the index/training time, query time and F1-score of the discovered communities. ACQ takes only 42.4 seconds and 852.7 seconds to build index on Reddit and Enlarge_Reddit. But in terms of the query time, it costs 32.2 milliseconds and 5726.6 milliseconds, while AQD-GNN only costs 6.7 milliseconds and 5.3", "block_bbox": [628, 1311, 1122, 1422], "block_id": 20, "block_order": 8, "group_id": 20, "block_polygon_points": [[628.0, 1311.0], [1122.0, 1311.0], [1122.0, 1422.0], [628.0, 1422.0]]}, {"block_label": "number", "block_content": "1253", "block_bbox": [588, 1507, 637, 1528], "block_id": 21, "block_order": null, "group_id": 21, "block_polygon_points": [[588.0, 1507.0], [636.0, 1507.0], [636.0, 1527.0], [588.0, 1527.0]]}], "layout_det_res": {"input_path":